---
/**
 * The full-bleed hero for article and case-study pages.
 *
 * ── THE PANORAMA IS A CONSEQUENCE, NOT A DECLARATION ─────────────────────────
 *
 * The brief reads as two designs — 16:9 on a phone, panoramic on a desktop — and
 * it is one, expressed in three properties:
 *
 *     inline-size: 100%      full bleed at every width
 *     aspect-ratio: 16/9     the shape while there is room for it
 *     max-block-size: 20rem  the height it is not allowed to exceed
 *
 * Below 568.9px the ratio governs. Above it the height pins at 320px while the
 * width keeps growing, so the box widens into 2.5:1 at 800, 4.5:1 at 1440 and
 * 6:1 at 1920 — the panorama, arrived at rather than specified.
 *
 * THE SWITCH POINT IS DERIVED, which is the reason this needs no breakpoint and
 * could not sensibly use one: 568.9 is wherever 16:9 happens to meet 20rem, so it
 * moves if either constant does. A media query would have to be recomputed by hand
 * every time; `max-block-size` simply cannot be wrong.
 *
 * THE CAP WAS 24rem UNTIL 2026-08-26 and every number above moved with it — the
 * switch point from 682.7, the ratios from 2.08/3.75/5. 20rem is what the Figma
 * Article board draws (320px against a 1440 frame), and phase 3 had transcribed the
 * wrong constant. Expect it to move again during fit and finish against real
 * content; when it does, edit `HERO_MAX_BLOCK_REM` below — that is the only value
 * to change — and recompute the four derived numbers in this comment.
 *
 * `object-fit: cover` fills the widening box, and `object-position` aims the crop
 * — see the hotspot note below.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS COSTS, AND WHY IT IS ACCEPTED ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Fetching at a flat 16/9 and displaying at up to 5:1 meant the browser threw most
 * of the file away — measured at 64% discarded on a real hero at a 1890px
 * viewport, because `object-fit: cover` scales the file 1.5x to fill the width and
 * crops the overflow.
 *
 * So the rungs are HEIGHT-CAPPED (`capHeight` in image.ts): each candidate comes
 * back already close to the shape its box will have, instead of tall and mostly
 * cropped. Same URLs, same breakpoint count — which is to say none — and roughly
 * half the bytes on the widest rungs.
 *
 * The trade it accepts is that rungs are no longer all the same shape. That is
 * safe HERE and nowhere else: this box is fixed by `aspect-ratio` and
 * `max-block-size` without consulting the file, and `object-fit: cover` absorbs
 * whatever arrives, so a candidate swap on resize cannot move the page. The same
 * option on <SanityImage> would reintroduce layout shift.
 *
 * ── IT WILL UPSCALE ON WIDE SCREENS, AND THAT IS INTENDED ────────────────────
 *
 * The srcset ladder stops at the source's own width, so a 1262px-wide asset offers
 * nothing above 1262w — and this element is full bleed, so on a 1890px viewport
 * the browser stretches that file to 1890 and the result is soft. At the 20rem cap
 * that is a 1890x320 box filled from an intrinsic 1262x640, still a 1.5x horizontal
 * stretch. (The 24rem version of this line recorded 1890x384 from 1262x710, measured
 * in DevTools. The pair above is RECOMPUTED from the new cap, not re-measured — the
 * top rung's height is min(1262 / (16/9), 640) — so treat it as arithmetic until
 * someone confirms it in a real browser.)
 *
 * THAT IS THE DESIGN, not a failure of the cap. Full bleed is not negotiable, so
 * the only question is which way to be wrong when a source is too small, and
 * stretching the largest REAL file is strictly better than asking Sanity to
 * upscale it first: identical on screen, and fewer bytes. The cap governs what is
 * requested; the CSS governs what is filled. They are not in tension.
 *
 * What it does buy is an authoring responsibility, Andy's by his own call
 * (2026-08-25): HERO SOURCES SHOULD BE BIG. Roughly 2880px wide covers both a
 * 2560px display at 1x and a 1440px laptop at 2x. Below that, wide screens get a
 * soft hero — gracefully, but visibly.
 *
 * ── LOADING, AND WHY IT INVERTS SanityImage's DEFAULTS ───────────────────────
 *
 * `sizes` is a flat `100vw` with no `auto`: this is full bleed, so its box IS the
 * viewport and a measured value could tell the browser nothing it does not
 * already know. `auto` would be invalid here regardless — it requires a lazy
 * image, and this one must not be.
 *
 * EAGER, against the default, with `fetchpriority="high"`. This is the largest
 * element above the fold on an article page, which makes it the Largest
 * Contentful Paint — lazy-loading it would defer the one image that metric is
 * measuring, and the priority hint moves it ahead of everything else competing
 * for the first connections.
 *
 * ── PLACEMENT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `inline-size: 100%` fills whatever contains it, so this belongs in the PAGE
 * STACK, never inside <Grid> — a grid would inset it to the content columns and
 * the full bleed would quietly disappear.
 *
 * At lg the hero swaps places with the page title. That is a reorder of the page
 * stack rather than anything this component knows about, and it is the page
 * template's job.
 */
import {imageAttrs, type SanityImageSource} from '../sanity/image'

interface Props {
  image: SanityImageSource
  alt?: string
  /** The shape at narrow widths, before the height cap takes over. */
  aspect?: string
  class?: string
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {image, alt, aspect = '16/9', class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props

/**
 * ── THE ONE NUMBER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The hero's height cap, in rem, and the only place it is written. Both consumers
 * derive from it: the stylesheet gets it as a custom property on the element, and
 * the srcset rung cap gets it in device pixels.
 *
 * IT USED TO BE TWO — a `HERO_MAX_BLOCK_PX` here and a `max-block-size: 20rem` in
 * the stylesheet, coupled by a "keep in step" comment. Comments are not a
 * mechanism, and this value is expected to keep moving through fit and finish, so
 * the two would eventually disagree by exactly one edit.
 *
 * The direction of the fix is forced. There is no way to read a CSS value from
 * here, so the frontmatter has to be the authority and the CSS has to receive it —
 * which is the same shape the hotspot already uses below, and the reason this needs
 * no new mechanism.
 *
 * ── WHAT THE `* 16` AND THE `* 2` ARE ────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 *   * 16   rem -> px at the NOMINAL root size. Not a measurement: a reader who has
 *          scaled their text up makes 20rem larger than 320px and the cap does not
 *          follow, leaving them a slightly soft hero — the same failure an
 *          undersized source already produces.
 *   * 2    the density hedge. A rung of width w may serve a w-wide viewport at 1x
 *          or a w/2-wide one at 2x, and the second needs twice the height. See
 *          `capHeight` in image.ts.
 *
 * So the px figure is a FETCH HEURISTIC derived from the design value, not a second
 * copy of it. That is why one constant can legitimately serve both.
 */
const HERO_MAX_BLOCK_REM = 20
const attrs = imageAttrs(image, {aspect, capHeight: HERO_MAX_BLOCK_REM * 16 * 2})

/**
 * THE HOTSPOT HAS TO WORK TWICE, because this image is cropped twice: once by
 * Sanity, to the 16/9 file, and again by `object-fit: cover` as the box widens
 * past it. The first is handled in the URL builder; this is the second.
 *
 * `object-position` takes the hotspot's centre, so the region an editor marked
 * stays framed as the panorama tightens. Without it the browser crops to the
 * middle and a subject that is not centred slides out of shot on wide screens —
 * silently, and only for some viewers.
 *
 * A custom property rather than `object-position` inline, so the property name and
 * its default stay in the stylesheet and only the DATA arrives from the document.
 *
 * PER-AXIS DEFAULTS, and the apparent duplication with the stylesheet's
 * `var(--hero-focus, 50% 50%)` is real but not redundant — the two cover different
 * cases. The CSS fallback handles NO HOTSPOT, where this emits nothing at all. The
 * `?? 0.5`s handle a hotspot that exists with a member missing, which Sanity's
 * schema permits and TypeGen therefore types as `number | undefined`. Defaulting
 * per axis rather than discarding the whole hotspot keeps a half-populated one
 * useful: a present `x` still aims the crop horizontally.
 *
 * A `!` assertion would compile and then write `NaN%` into the style attribute.
 */
const hotspot = image?.hotspot
const focus = hotspot
  ? `--hero-focus: ${((hotspot.x ?? 0.5) * 100).toFixed(2)}% ${((hotspot.y ?? 0.5) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`
  : null

/**
 * Both custom properties in one attribute — the design constant and, when there is
 * one, the hotspot. `filter(Boolean)` rather than a ternary because the hotspot is
 * genuinely optional while the height never is.
 *
 * THE STYLESHEET HAS NO FALLBACK for `--hero-max-block`, deliberately. A
 * `var(--hero-max-block, 20rem)` would put the number back in two places, which is
 * the whole thing this replaces. The consequence is that the property MUST arrive:
 * an unresolvable `var()` in `max-block-size` computes to `none`, so the cap would
 * silently disappear and the hero would run at 16/9 to any width. That is what the
 * attribute order in the template guards.
 */
const style = [`--hero-max-block: ${HERO_MAX_BLOCK_REM}rem`, focus].filter(Boolean).join('; ')

const resolvedAlt = alt ?? image?.altText ?? ''
if (alt === undefined && !image?.altText && attrs) {
  console.warn(`[SanityHero] no altText on ${image?.asset?._ref} — rendering alt=""`)
}
---

{
  attrs && (
    <img
      class:list={['hero', className]}
      src={attrs.src}
      srcset={attrs.srcset}
      sizes="100vw"
      width={attrs.width}
      height={attrs.height}
      alt={resolvedAlt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
      decoding="async"
      {...rest}
      style={style}
    />
  )
}

<style>
  .hero {
    inline-size: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;

    /* 20rem = 320px, matching the Figma Article board — but the number is NOT here.
       It arrives from `HERO_MAX_BLOCK_REM` in the frontmatter, which is also what
       caps the srcset rungs, so the two cannot drift. Component-local rather than a
       token: one consumer, and DESIGN.md names no chrome height. In rem so it
       tracks a reader's text size, like the rest of the type-derived spacing.

       NO FALLBACK VALUE ON PURPOSE — see the note on `style` in the frontmatter. A
       second number here would defeat the point, and its absence is what makes the
       attribute order in the template load-bearing rather than incidental. */
    max-block-size: var(--hero-max-block);

    /* Fills the box as it widens past 16/9. Without this the image would letterbox
       or distort once the height caps. */
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: var(--hero-focus, 50% 50%);

    /* base.css sets `height: auto` on every image, which is what lets the
       width/height attributes reserve a box from their ratio. Here the CSS
       determines the box outright, so this component cannot shift the page at
       all — the attributes are belt to its braces rather than the mechanism. */
  }
</style>
